// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
; specs/turbobaby/request_identity.t27 -- who a request is, and what a failed proof means
; This contract owns the repository's OWN decisions inside the Telegram WebApp initData check:
; which keys leave the data-check string, the input size cap, the freshness window, the refusal
; of a future-dated stamp, which hash comparisons may admit, the gate names and their
; precedence, the per-IP bound on failed admin attempts, and the rule that a failed proof yields
; NO user rather than a default one. Every number below is read from src/api/auth.rs and carries
; its line. ASCII only (L3), English only (LANG-EN).
; --
; This file is a ROOT of the ownership graph. Like availability.t27 it has no outbound reuse
; edge: it takes no decision from another contract, and every domain fact it could have restated
; is an input boolean instead. The direction is written the other way round, in CONSUMERS below,
; so a reader can grep who depends on identity without opening the other 42 files. That sentence
; said "twelve" until 2026-09-21, which was the corpus size on the day it was written and has not
; been the corpus size since; the array it points at was re-measured the same day.
; --
; WHY (measured 2026-09-20, re-measured 2026-09-21). grep -ril "initData|init_data|hmac" over the
; twelve contracts that existed before this one returned 0 files, and that zero is the finding
; this contract was written for: the corpus said what the product answers and not one line of it
; said who is asking.
; CORRECTED 2026-09-21, and what is being retired is a FALSE ABSENCE, which is the worst thing a
; reader can inherit from this corpus because it is the one claim nobody re-runs. This paragraph
; used to continue "and re-run over specs/ today it still returns only this file". Re-run today
; over all 43 files it returns SIX: bot_surface.t27, client_errors.t27, events_booking.t27,
; runtime_config.t27, webapp_bridge.t27 and this one. The claim that was ever load-bearing
; survives the correction and is narrower than the one that rotted -- not one of those five
; restates the data-check decision, and each names a different half of the subject.
; bot_surface measures that the BOT gate does not verify initData at all; client_errors records
; that initData-shaped tokens are stripped from free text before storage; events_booking says a
; booking row comes from validated initData; runtime_config reads a handle out of initDataUnsafe
; in the UI; and webapp_bridge owns where the string COMES FROM on the client and names this
; contract for the byte cap and the admission rule instead of copying either. Measured the same
; day and still exclusive: INIT_DATA_MAX_BYTES and init_data_size_admits are each declared exactly
; once in specs/, here, and 4096 appears nowhere else as a cap.
; The consumers below name this owner by ID and none of them restates the check. Behind
; that silence sit 113 gate call sites in 18 modules -- 17 under src/api plus src/main.rs:1181 --
; and by gate 84 check_admin, 23 check_owner, 6 check_owner_lenient, every one of them depending
; on the answer. The check is not the generic one from the protocol documentation either. Its
; central decision is local and was paid for: Telegram's `signature` key is a third-party Ed25519
; field, it is present in every Mini App initData now, it is NOT part of the HMAC data-check
; string, and leaving it in makes the HMAC never match. The code records that as the root cause
; of a permanent 401 across the gated surface (src/api/auth.rs:31-34, the removal at :148). A
; contract that forgets it re-earns a total outage, which is why the removed-key set is the first
; thing declared here.
; --
; WHERE THE BRIEF IS REFUTED. The survey brief cites ~414 for "the X-Admin-Token fallback, and
; whether a localhost bypass exists". Line 414 is the signature of verify_admin_token, the
; constant-time comparator for an already-supplied token. The fallback is a different thing in a
; different place: it is step 2 of check_admin at :476-477, and it is reached even when a VALID
; initData names a non-admin id (:462-468, the comment at :467), which is the part a reader of
; :414 would never learn. The no-localhost-bypass claim is real but is a comment, at :8; a sweep
; of src/api/auth.rs and src/api/admin.rs for localhost, 127.0.0.1 and bypass returns that one
; line and no code, so the absence is measured rather than promised.
; --
; WHERE THE BRIEF IS REFUTED A SECOND TIME, and the guarantee gets weaker. The brief says
; check_owner_lenient at ~602 "verifies auth_date and the id WITHOUT the HMAC". That function
; does not verify anything. Its whole body is check_owner(headers, state, expected_telegram_id)
; (:602-609); its _metric_kind argument is ignored by name (:606). The lenient verification is
; lenient_owner_verify (:634) reading extract_init_data_user_id_and_auth_date (:613), and
; check_owner reaches it ITSELF after strict HMAC failure (:596). So leniency is not an opt-in
; that six call sites chose. It is on for all 23 check_owner call sites, and the difference is
; the whole guarantee: read as the brief has it, an unsigned request is admitted only where a
; caller asked for it; read as the code has it, an unsigned request with a matching id and a
; fresh stamp is admitted everywhere an owner is checked. That is pinned below as a tripwire,
; because a contract that states the strict path and omits this one would be a false guarantee
; with a passing test suite.
; --
; WHAT THIS FILE NEVER DOES. It carries no bot token, no admin password, no admin token, no
; derived secret and no admin id: a spec naming a real admin id would be precisely the leak this
; gate exists to prevent, so membership arrives as the boolean id_is_configured_admin and the
; list stays in runtime configuration (read at src/api/auth.rs:455). It does not restate
; HMAC-SHA256 as arithmetic -- that is an external standard, and the secret-key derivation is
; cited at :185 rather than repeated. It does not map endpoints to gates: 113 call sites over 18
; modules have no single owner, so only the gate names and their precedence are declared. It
; owns no read-transport status code -- 200, 304, 400, 404 and 500 are turbobaby/catalog-api's,
; where they are declared as HTTP_OK, HTTP_NOT_MODIFIED, HTTP_BAD_REQUEST, HTTP_NOT_FOUND and
; HTTP_INTERNAL_ERROR. SCOPED HONESTLY on re-measurement, 2026-09-21: those five are that
; contract's as the READ transport's answers, and not exclusively as numerals. 400 is also
; declared by order_status.t27, upload_media.t27 and cart_persistence.t27, 200 and 404 by
; publication.t27, and NINE contracts in all declare a transport status of their own, so "belong
; to" was the wrong verb for a corpus this size. The nine were counted by reading every u16
; constant in specs/ whose value is a three-digit IANA code, not by grepping the HTTP_ prefix: the
; prefix answers six and silently drops cart_persistence.t27, happy_hour.t27 and
; webapp_bridge.t27, which spell their statuses CART_WRITE_BAD_REQUEST, STATUS_OK and
; SERVER_REPLAY_STATUS. cart_persistence.t27 is worth naming twice, because its own comment says
; it declares three refusal numerals "for the same reason turbobaby/request-identity declares its
; own three" -- it is this file's precedent quoting this file. What IS exclusive is the half this file depends on, and it is re-verified
; rather than remembered: 401, 403 and 429 are declared here and at no other line of specs/, so
; the three refusal statuses a failed proof produces are still this contract's alone. It restates
; no privacy field list, and that pair of homes is re-verified the same day: the D14 enumerations
; live in exactly two files and they are the two named here -- FORBIDDEN_FIELDS in
; specs/agents/turbobaby.t27 and FORBIDDEN_UNIT_FIELDS in specs/turbobaby/bike_catalog.t27 -- and
; no renter name, handle, phone, key code, plate, debt, cost or personal note appears here in any
; form, fixtures included. A user id is never written down; it is only ever compared.
; phi^2 + 1/phi^2 = 3 | TRINITY

module turbobaby-request-identity;

pub const KIND : str = "identity";
pub const ID : str = "turbobaby/request-identity";
pub const NAME : str = "Request identity and proof outcomes";
pub const SOURCE : str = "src/api/auth.rs";
pub const MEASURED_AT : str = "2026-09-20";

; --- Root of the ownership graph ----------------------------------------------------------------
; No REUSES_* constant exists in this file and that is the claim, not an omission. The consumers
; are named by ID because there are no imports, and they carry two different strengths of
; evidence, which is why the count of the stronger kind is its own constant.
; RE-MEASURED 2026-09-21, and the array below grew from eight entries to twenty-two. The list was
; written when twelve contracts existed; fourteen more have landed since and every one of them
; declares the edge. The command is the whole method and can be re-run by anyone:
;   grep -rlE "^pub const REUSES_REQUEST_IDENTITY_ID" specs/
; It answers 18 files, and those 18 are positions 0-3 and 8-21 below, each holding
; REUSES_REQUEST_IDENTITY_ID = "turbobaby/request-identity" as a constant a grep finds from both
; ends. THE DECLARATION IS WHAT IS COUNTED, not the mention. Grepping for the bare ID string
; instead answers MORE files and is the wrong measurement for this array: some contracts name this
; owner in prose without declaring an edge to it, which is a legal thing to do and not a
; twenty-third consumer. That looser count is left out of the constants on purpose -- it moves
; whenever any contract writes a sentence, and a number that moves for reasons the array does not
; care about is a number that will be wrong before it is read.
; A CLOSED array that has stopped being closed is the defect this re-measurement exists to
; kill: the old eight were not wrong, they were all that had been written, and a reader trusting
; the count would have concluded that fourteen live edges do not exist.
; THE TWO STRENGTHS OF EVIDENCE, and the ORDER of the array, which is now load-bearing.
; Positions 0-3 are the four that declared the edge first (catalog-write, game-score, star-award,
; order-status). order_status was written in parallel with this file and promoted itself from a
; prose mention to a declared edge, which is why it was counted here from the beginning.
; Positions 4-7 are the four that are MEASURED instead, from gate call sites in the module that
; implements their subject: commerce -> src/api/cart.rs (owner gate) and src/api/orders.rs (both
; gates); bike-catalog -> src/api/catalog.rs and src/api/bikes.rs (admin gate); availability ->
; the unit rows written through src/api/bikes.rs behind the same gate; ride-game ->
; src/api/game.rs (the owner gate, entered through check_owner_lenient at :96). That half of the
; list is by module, never by endpoint, and no constant on their side names it even now.
; Positions 8-21 are the fourteen found on 2026-09-21, and every one of them is a DECLARED edge.
; The four measured-only entries are kept where they are rather than sorted to the end, because
; moving them would change the index every existing assertion below is written against, and an
; index that moves is the same defect as a line number that moves.
; Each sibling is cited by its ID and by the DECLARATION NAME it holds, never by a line in its
; file: the four citations that used to stand here were line numbers into catalog_write.t27,
; game_score.t27, star_award.t27 and order_status.t27, and by 2026-09-21 they no longer landed on
; the constant they named.
pub const IS_OWNERSHIP_ROOT : bool = true;
pub const REUSE_EDGE_COUNT : u8 = 0;
; Re-measured 2026-09-26: 23, with turbobaby/referral-credit (the referral credit of R3, which
; declares the edge) appended at position 22 so that no earlier index moves.
pub const CONSUMER_COUNT : u8 = 23;
pub const CONSUMERS_DECLARING_THE_EDGE : u8 = 19;
pub const CONSUMER_COUNT_BEFORE_2026_09_26 : u8 = 22;
pub const CONSUMERS_ADDED_2026_09_26 : u8 = 1;
pub const CONSUMERS_MEASURED_ONLY : u8 = 4;
pub const CONSUMER_CENSUS_MEASURED_AT : str = "2026-09-21";
pub const CONSUMER_CENSUS_GREP : str = "grep -rlE \"^pub const REUSES_REQUEST_IDENTITY_ID\" specs/";
pub const THE_CENSUS_COUNTS_DECLARATIONS_NOT_MENTIONS : bool = true;
pub const EDGE_DECLARATION_NAME : str = "REUSES_REQUEST_IDENTITY_ID";
pub const CONSUMER_COUNT_BEFORE_THE_RE_MEASUREMENT : u8 = 8;
pub const CONSUMERS : [23]str = [
    "turbobaby/catalog-write",
    "turbobaby/game-score",
    "turbobaby/star-award",
    "turbobaby/order-status",
    "turbobaby/commerce",
    "turbobaby/bike-catalog",
    "turbobaby/availability",
    "turbobaby/ride-game",
    "turbobaby/api-surface",
    "turbobaby/bot-surface",
    "turbobaby/cart-persistence",
    "turbobaby/checkout-contact",
    "turbobaby/client-errors",
    "turbobaby/deeplink",
    "turbobaby/events-booking",
    "turbobaby/loyalty-ledger",
